## Explicit Early Cleanup with mem.drop

`mem.drop(value)` explicitly drops a value before the end of its scope. This is useful for releasing resources early.

### Basic mem.drop

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(1);
  data.push(2);

  // Use data...
  let len = data.len();

  mem.drop(data);  // explicitly drop data NOW
  // data is dead — cannot use it anymore
  // let x = data.len();  // ERROR: data was dropped

  return len;
}
```

### Why Use mem.drop?

- Release large memory allocations early
- Close file handles or network connections promptly
- Enforce that a value cannot be used after a certain point
- Satisfy borrow checker requirements

### mem.drop vs Scope End

```xiom
fn without_drop() -> Int {
  let big = Vec[Int]();
  // push lots of data...
  // compute something quick
  // big holds memory until end of function
  return 0;
}

fn with_drop() -> Int {
  let big = Vec[Int]();
  // push lots of data...
  let result = big.len();
  mem.drop(big);  // release memory NOW
  // rest of function runs without big's memory
  // ... more work ...
  return result;
}
```

### mem.drop and Ownership

`mem.drop` takes ownership and destroys the value:

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(42);
mem.drop(v);  // v's ownership moves into drop — destroyed
// v is invalid after this point
```

### When NOT to Use

In most cases, let the compiler handle drop. Only use `mem.drop` when you need explicit control.

